 reasons may say I  actually thrive in this environment and  I want to make school visitations I want  to take advantage of some of the  programs athletics etc but I feel safer  at least for a the beginning of the year  based on parental concerns staying home  and learning from a distance what I just  described this sort of a spectrum of  opportunity that is choice driven  identifies the needs of parents and  students and tries to match them with  individualized learning pathways there  are suited and suitable for each kid and  I think that's what's going to explode  it's sort of the new dimension of choice  in America the super powerful because if  you take it one intellectual step from  there that means that even the physical  experiences will have to support the  virtual and that to some degree you're  not necessarily bound to one physical  location anymore in theory that is  absolutely right and  I'm already writing letters and  advocating based on the experience we've  had I think state houses and the federal  government needs to rethink a number of  things number one how education is  funded I mean I think we're proving that  having everybody start at the same time  in at the same time for the exact same  number of minutes every single day in  the same place doesn't work anymore  so we need to liberalize funding  methodology for public education across  the country so that mandated seat time  is not a mandatory feature in in student  funding secondly the how when how by by  whom and how often something is taught  and what is thought needs to be looked  at as well and and I think that what we  have learned under duress  right in the rush can now over the  summer any months to come be analyzed  and let's use some of the learnings from  this experience to actually enhance the  student experience moving forward and I  think that's reasonably powerful Wow  well you know superintendent Carvalho I  could talk to you for a few more hours  actually want to take notes on a lot of  this because what you're doing I mean at  a very large scale with a complex School  District and there's a lot more house  there's a lot of questions how are you  able to pull this off you know what  you're saying makes so much intellectual  sense but you know the timeframes and  obviously the dollars where are you  getting the resources to do it  you know I guess you're willing to stake  your job on a lot of things we talked  about that so that's the will I mean I  actually just answered a few if you have  a couple of more minutes I'd love you  know how are you able to do this how are  you able to do these programs you where  you finding the resources what you're  talking about places are like oh we  don't have an extra penny we're already  you know over our heads in terms of  budget yeah well you know I think real  number one let's recognize that the the  fiscal shock that our nation is about to  experience the fiscal shock that the  public education systems across America  are about to face is going to be a  daunting one but we've been there before  probably not to this degree so you know  I don't want to give Tallahassee Mike  or Washington DC a pass during the last  economic recession under stimulus  packages back then over one hundred  fifteen billion dollars were  appropriated to stabilize educational  opportunities across the country so far  under the cares act the current federal  stimulus package is only sixteen billion  dollars have been earmarked and trust me  by all accounts you know and we read  Moody's Analytics as often as we  possibly can the prediction is that  what's coming is a more powerful  longer-lasting economic recession than  the Great Recession of 2008 9 so the  federal government needs to do quite a  bit more at the same time I'm one who  believes that that reductions across the  board don't require leadership so even  during tough times  you ought to prioritize that which is  most important and that's where we start  we follow something we branded here in  Miami which is values based zero based  budgeting each year each year starts at  zero with the highest priorities being  funded first everything else a second  there and that's exactly what we're  doing we are preparing for for this  fiscal shock so we turned off the you  know the the spending rivers long ago  non-essential hiring stopped procurement  stopped renegotiation of private sector  contracts at a discount 10% minimally  because whatever cost whatever the cost  was for something a service or a good  three months ago it's not the same today  you know as a nation we went from 3%  unemployment to over 20 percent in six  weeks everything has changed which means  we need to change so we will face the  fiscal crisis we have and have amassed  reasonable reserves and we pass two Bond  referenda that have secured teacher  salaries in a very significant way we  boosted our teacher salaries last year  anywhere from 14 to 23 percent and  include security  that's not in danger because the funding  stream for that is property tax based  not state revenue so that's gonna remain  stable our investment technology is  going to remain stable or upkeep of our  school is going to remain stable because  we saw the need to help ourselves  operations yes we will depend on state  and federal revenues and we're working  those as well  but there's always an opportunity to  prioritize that which is important in a  budget we don't believe in five percent  across the board ten percent across the  board in fact during times of recession  it is customary for us to actually  augment funding for certain critical  priorities like what we're doing for the  fragile student population rather than  shying away from it  obviously other areas will have to be  cut but those are judgement calls in a  basis of priorities and and that's what  leaders right now in America should be  doing you know what's most important  what are the priorities what are the  gaps that if 